export const DIALECT_SOURCES: Readonly<Record<string, string>> = {
  "org.dxos.spec.app": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.app\nname: DSL.App\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe app dialect: vocabulary for describing a Composer application's\n**navigation and presentation structure** — app-graph nodes, deck level\nchains, planks, companions, surface contributions, and menus. It complements\nthe standard dialect (`type`, `op`, `component`, `service`, `feat`, `test`),\nwhich describes data and behavior; this dialect describes *where things\nappear and how the user reaches them*.\n\nConsumers list the terms they use in their Extensions table (each resolves to\na URI defined here), or extend this document wholesale via frontmatter\n`extends`. See `packages/plugins/plugin-inbox/PLUGIN.mdl` for a full\nreal-world usage.\n\n## Model\n\nThe vocabulary mirrors the runtime exactly:\n\n- The **app-graph** is a reactive graph of nodes contributed by plugins.\n  A `node` block declares a node (or family of nodes) — its subject, its\n  children (e.g. folders), and the actions that hang off it.\n- The **deck** renders planks; a plank is *just a graph-node id* whose content\n  resolves through an `article` Surface dispatch. A `deck` block declares a\n  **level chain** (`Mailbox > Message > Attachment`): opening at a level\n  reuses that level's plank and closes levels below.\n- A **companion** is a child node (`~variant` linked segment) rendered as a\n  plank attached to its context plank.\n- A **surface** contribution maps `(role, subject type)` to a component — the\n  single dispatch mechanism by which anything renders anywhere.\n- A **menu** declares toolbar/context-menu content as references to\n  operations (`op:` blocks) and graph-node actions — never inline behavior.\n\n### Reference forms\n\nCross-references follow the core rules (`[id]`, `op:Name`, `component:Name`)\nwith these additional prefixes: `node:Name`, `surface:Name`, `type:Name`.\nSubjects reference ECHO types declared in `type` blocks (or external types\nlisted in prose).\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term        | URI                           |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `node`      | `org.dxos.mdl.node@1.0`      |\n| `deck`      | `org.dxos.mdl.deck@1.0`      |\n| `plank`     | `org.dxos.mdl.plank@1.0`     |\n| `companion` | `org.dxos.mdl.companion@1.0` |\n| `surface`   | `org.dxos.mdl.surface@1.0`   |\n| `menu`      | `org.dxos.mdl.menu@1.0`      |\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next node\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.node@1.0\n  desc: |\n    An app-graph node contribution: a node (or per-object family of nodes)\n    the plugin adds to the graph. `source` names the parent — a well-known\n    node id (e.g. the space root) or a type whose instances the connector\n    expands. Children declared inline become child nodes (e.g. folders).\n    Actions reference `op:` blocks and surface as menu/toolbar items wherever\n    the node appears.\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    source?: NodeRef | TypeRef   # parent node or the type this connector expands\n    subject?: TypeRef | Literal  # the node's data (an ECHO object or a constant)\n    properties?: FieldMap        # label, icon, disposition, position, ...\n    children?: NodeList          # inline child node declarations (folders etc.)\n    actions?: OpRefList          # operations contributed to this node\n    article?: SurfaceRef | ComponentRef  # what an article surface renders for it\n  nesting: self\n```\n\n```mdl\next deck\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.deck@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A declared level chain: how reading down a hierarchy maps onto planks.\n    Each level names the plank that opens at that depth; opening at a level\n    reuses that level's plank and closes deeper ones. Modifier gestures\n    (meta/ctrl) opt out and open a free plank instead.\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    chain: PlankRefChain         # ordered, e.g. Mailbox > Message > Attachment\n```\n\n```mdl\next plank\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.plank@1.0\n  desc: |\n    One deck plank kind: which subject it presents and through which article.\n    `parent` places it in a level chain (equivalently declared via `deck`);\n    `companions` lists the companion variants available on it.\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    subject: TypeRef             # the graph node data this plank presents\n    article: SurfaceRef | ComponentRef\n    parent?: PlankRef            # the level above in the chain\n    companions?: CompanionRefList\n```\n\n```mdl\next companion\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.companion@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A plank companion: a child node (`~variant` linked segment) rendered as a\n    plank attached to its context plank, sharing its attention context.\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    of: TypeRef | NodeRef        # the context plank's subject\n    variant: Identifier          # the `~variant` segment / tab id\n    component?: ComponentRef | SurfaceRef\n    properties?: FieldMap        # label, icon, position, ...\n```\n\n```mdl\next surface\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.surface@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A Surface contribution: maps a role token plus a subject-type filter to a\n    component. This is the app's single presentation dispatch — articles,\n    sections, cards, dialogs, and companions all resolve through it.\n\n    Resolution is not most-specific-wins: every contribution whose role and\n    filter match is a candidate, and `position` orders them (`first` /\n    `last` are the sentinels; equal positions keep registration order). A\n    role-only contribution therefore competes with a subject-specific one,\n    and the subject-specific one must claim an earlier position to win.\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    role: Identifier             # role token (article | section | card | dialog | ...)\n    subject?: TypeRef            # type filter; omitted = role-only match\n    component: ComponentRef\n    variant?: Identifier\n    position?: number | first | last  # tie-break among all matching contributions\n```\n\n```mdl\next menu\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.menu@1.0\n  desc: |\n    Toolbar / context-menu content for a node or type: ordered references to\n    operations and action groups. Menus carry no behavior of their own —\n    every item resolves to an `op:` (directly or through a graph-node\n    action).\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    of: TypeRef | NodeRef        # what the menu attaches to\n    disposition?: toolbar | context | list-item\n    items: OpRefList             # ordered; groups as nested lists\n```\n\n## Example\n\nA mailbox with system folders, a two-level reading chain, and a companion:\n\n```mdl\nnode MailboxNode\n  source: type:Mailbox\n  subject: type:Mailbox\n  properties:\n    icon: ph--tray--regular\n  children:\n    - Inbox    # folder: system-tag filtered view of the mailbox\n    - Sent\n    - Drafts\n  actions: [op:SyncMailbox, op:DraftEmail]\n```\n\n```mdl\nplank MailboxPlank\n  subject: type:Mailbox\n  article: component:MailboxArticle\n```\n\n```mdl\nplank MessagePlank\n  subject: type:Message\n  article: component:MessageArticle\n  parent: MailboxPlank\n```\n\n```mdl\ndeck MailboxDeck\n  desc: Reading down the mailbox reuses one plank per level (two levels shown; the attachment level is elided).\n  chain: MailboxPlank > MessagePlank\n```\n\n```mdl\ncompanion RelatedContacts\n  of: type:Message\n  variant: contacts\n  component: component:RelatedContacts\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.changelog": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.changelog\nname: DSL.Changelog\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe changelog dialect. Adds a `## Changelog` section to a spec: an append-only log of every\ndecision that changed it, each one attributed to the person or the agent that made it. The body of\na spec says what is true now; the changelog says who decided it, when, and why.\n\nA spec is written by people and agents together. Typically the person gives coarse direction and\nthe agent fills in the gaps. The two carry different authority, so the log keeps their knowledge\nin separate entries: every entry names exactly one decider, typed as `user:` or `agent:`, and\n\"which parts of this spec did a person choose?\" is a filter, not an archaeology project.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term       | URI                          |\n| ---------- | ---------------------------- |\n| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0` |\n| `question` | `org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0` |\n\n## Actors\n\nEvery actor reference carries one of two prefixes. The prefix is the point of the dialect: the\nlinter rejects a `by:` without one.\n\n| Form              | Who                                                   | Example                       |\n| ----------------- | ----------------------------------------------------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `user:<handle>`   | A person, by GitHub username or email.                | `user:alice`, `user:a@b.org` |\n| `agent:<name>`    | An agent, by harness or product name — never a model. | `agent:claude-code`           |\n\nName an agent by what ran it (`agent:claude-code`, `agent:composer-assistant`), not by the model\nthat served it: models change mid-session and the name must stay stable across the log.\n\n## Rules\n\n1. **Every change to a spec adds a decision.** A change to anything outside `## Changelog` comes\n   with at least one new entry in the same change. The check\n   `pnpm deus changelog check` enforces this in CI. Trivial edits are exempt:\n   whitespace, and fixes of at most 3 words by 1–2 letters each, excluding words with digits and\n   normative words. A bulk mechanical edit, or a change with no spec decision behind it (rule 9),\n   may be waived with a `Spec-Changelog-Skip: <reason>` commit trailer.\n2. **One decider per entry.** An entry is `by:` exactly one `user:` or `agent:`. Knowledge from\n   two sources is two entries, even when they land in the same edit.\n3. **A person's input is recorded faithfully, first.** When a person gives direction, the first\n   entry is `by: user:<them>` and restates what they said in spec terms. The recording agent may\n   resolve references (\"it\", \"the canvas\" → `Scene`), include the context needed to read the entry\n   on its own, and fix wording. It must not add or remove information: no requirement, rationale or\n   assumption the person did not give, and nothing they said left out. Prompts are messy, so the\n   entry is a clean statement of their intent, not a transcript. The decider is who chose, not who\n   typed: an agent writes this entry, the person made it.\n4. **What the agent adds is a separate entry.** Gaps the agent filled — shapes, names, edge cases,\n   a reading of an ambiguous instruction — go in later `by: agent:<name>` entries, one per\n   independent choice, referring to the entry they build on (`Fills in [D-1]`).\n5. **An agent entry names its principal.** `for:` is the person whose instruction the agent was\n   acting under. It is required on `agent:` entries and forbidden on `user:` entries.\n6. **Approval is a decision.** A person who reviews and accepts agent entries makes a new\n   `user:` entry saying so (\"Approve [D-2] and [D-3].\"). Nothing in the approved entry changes.\n7. **Append-only.** Never edit or delete an entry. To reverse or refine one, append a new entry\n   with `supersedes: D-n`. The superseded entry stays — it is the trace.\n8. **Identifiers are monotonic, newest last.** `D-1`, `D-2`, … in the order appended. Never reuse\n   or renumber.\n9. **Only spec decisions.** An entry records a choice about the specified system: its behavior,\n   requirements, structure, interfaces, data, tests, or the reason for one of those. How the authors\n   worked is not a decision: which tool or skill they used, how the spec is shared or reviewed, which\n   PR a change goes in, that the document exists, has diagrams, or was reorganized. When a person's\n   instruction is only about process, follow it and record nothing. A spec change with no decision\n   behind it carries the `Spec-Changelog-Skip` trailer instead. `.agents/skills/mdl/scripts/check-decisions.ts` checks\n   new entries with TypeSafe System One.\n10. **An open question is a `question`, and its answer is a decision.** A choice the spec needs but\n    nobody has made yet goes in `## Changelog` as `question Q-n`, attributed like a decision. It is\n    answered by appending a `decision` whose `answers:` names it; the decider is whoever answered.\n    The question itself is never edited, so \"answered\" is derived, not stored. Only the first\n    decision that answers a question counts; to change the answer, supersede that decision.\n    Question ids are `Q-1`, `Q-2`, … in the order asked, a sequence of their own.\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next decision\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0\n  desc: |\n    One decision about the spec, attributed to exactly one actor. The inline prose is the decision\n    itself, stated as what is now true (\"Bounds are derived, never stored.\"), not as the edit\n    that was made.\n  fields:\n    date: Date                  # YYYY-MM-DD, the day the decision was made\n    by: ActorRef                # user:<handle> | agent:<name> — the decider\n    for?: UserRef               # agent entries only: the person the agent acted for\n    affects?: RefList           # block ids the decision constrains, e.g. [Scene, F-2.1]\n    supersedes?: Identifier     # an earlier decision this one replaces\n    why?: Prose                 # the reason; on a user entry, only a reason the person gave\n    rejected?: ProseList        # alternatives considered and why they lost\n    source?: URI                # PR, review comment, issue, or session where it was made\n    answers?: Identifier        # a question this decision answers, e.g. Q-1\n  inline-prose: true\n```\n\n```mdl\next question\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A choice the spec needs that nobody has made yet, attributed to the actor that asked it. The\n    inline prose is the question. It is answered by a later `decision` whose `answers:` names it.\n  fields:\n    date: Date                  # YYYY-MM-DD, the day it was asked\n    by: ActorRef                # user:<handle> | agent:<name> — who asked\n    for?: UserRef               # agent questions only: the person the agent acted for\n    affects?: RefList           # block ids the answer will constrain\n    context?: Prose             # why it is asked, and what depends on the answer\n    options?: ProseList         # suggested answers, each `<title>: <description>`\n    source?: URI                # PR, review comment, issue, or session where it was asked\n  inline-prose: true\n```\n\n## Example\n\nA person asks for a feature in one line; the agent records it, then fills it in; the person\napproves part of it and overrides the rest.\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-1: The `Scene` canvas grows to fit its content when a node moves past its edge.\n  date: 2026-10-02\n  by: user:alice\n  affects: [Scene]\n  source: https://github.com/dxos/dxos/pull/<n>\n```\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-2: Bounds are derived from content, never stored. Fills in [D-1].\n  date: 2026-10-02\n  by: agent:claude-code\n  for: user:alice\n  affects: [Scene]\n  why: Stored bounds drift from the content whenever a node moves outside an edit path.\n```\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-3: Bounds grow to the major grid. Fills in [D-1].\n  date: 2026-10-02\n  by: agent:claude-code\n  for: user:alice\n  affects: [Scene, F-2.1]\n  why: Growing to the minor grid re-lays the canvas on every drag step.\n  rejected:\n    - Fixed padding: clips large nodes at the edge.\n```\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-4: Approve [D-2].\n  date: 2026-10-05\n  by: user:alice\n```\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-5: Bounds grow to the minor grid while dragging and snap to the major grid on drop, because growing to the major grid feels laggy.\n  date: 2026-10-05\n  by: user:alice\n  supersedes: D-3\n```\n\nThe agent meets a choice it should not make alone, and asks; the person's answer is a decision.\n\n```mdl\nquestion Q-1: Does the canvas shrink when nodes are removed?\n  date: 2026-10-06\n  by: agent:claude-code\n  for: user:alice\n  affects: [Scene]\n  context: [D-1] grows the bounds; nothing says whether they ever shrink.\n  options:\n    - Never: bounds only grow, so the view never jumps under the reader.\n    - On drop: bounds fit the content again after each edit.\n```\n\n```mdl\ndecision D-6: Never.\n  date: 2026-10-07\n  by: user:alice\n  answers: Q-1\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.core": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.core\nname: DSL.Core\nversion: 1.0\n---\n\nThe minimal base language. Defines document format and the `ext` primitive only.\nAll domain constructs — type, op, feat, scenario, test, ui, db, service, etc. — are extensions.\n\n## Document Format\n\nA `.mdl` file has three parts:\n\n1. **Frontmatter** — YAML block delimited by `---`. Contains document metadata.\n2. **Body** — Standard Markdown prose, headings, and lists.\n3. **Blocks** — Typed fenced sections carrying structured declarations.\n\n### Frontmatter\n\n```\nid:        URI           # globally unique document identifier\nname:      Identifier    # human-readable name\nversion:   SemVer        # document version\nextends?:  URI[]         # specs this document extends (transitive)\n```\n\n### Blocks\n\nAll blocks use the `mdl` fence. The first line of the body declares the block\ntype, optional identifier, and optional title:\n\n````\n```mdl\n<type> [<id>][: <title>]\n  key: value\n  key?: TypeExpr\n  key: a | b | c\n```\n````\n\n- `<type>` must be declared in the Extensions section of this document or an ancestor.\n- `<id>` is an optional locally-scoped identifier (e.g. `F-1.1`, `Board`).\n- `<title>` is optional human-readable prose after the colon.\n- Block identifiers must be unique within the document.\n\n### Block Body Syntax\n\nWithin a block, the body is a sequence of key-value pairs:\n\n```\nkey: value                # scalar\nkey: a | b | c            # union list (literals)\nkey?: TypeExpr            # optional field declaration\nkey:                      # multi-line value\n  - item one\n  - item two\n# comment\n```\n\nWhen an extension declares `inline-prose: true`, prose may appear between\nthe header line and the first key-value pair:\n\n````\n```mdl\nfeat F-1: Start Game\n  A new Board is created with pieces in starting positions.  ← inline prose\n  req F-1.1: White moves first.\n```\n````\n\n### Cross-References\n\nAny declared block identifier may be referenced elsewhere in the document as `[<id>]`.\nThe linter verifies that all references resolve to a declared block.\n\n### External References\n\nA spec names a block of another spec as `<spec id>#<item>`, where the item is a block id or `req` id\n(`com.acme.canvas#F-3.1`), and declares that spec under `## Dependencies` (below). A\nblock may name code, or anything else outside the specs, by **source key**:\n`github:<owner>/<repo>/<path>[#<fragment>]`, with the path relative to the repository root.\n\n| Key names           | Example                                                        | Fragment            |\n| ------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------- | ------------------- |\n| A code symbol       | `github:dxos/dxos/packages/sdk/types/src/types/Spec.ts#make`   | Symbol (`A.b` ok)   |\n| A whole file        | `github:dxos/dxos/packages/apps/composer-app/vite.config.ts`   | none                |\n\nA spec does not name the code that implements it: those links are annotations kept outside the\nspec (a review's `evidence:`, see `review.mdl`), and the spec viewer renders them as decorations\nbeside the items they annotate. Tools resolve keys against a local checkout of the repository. A\nkey is a location, not a version: it names whatever the checkout holds.\n\n---\n\n## Extensions Section\n\nEvery document that uses non-core block types MUST declare them:\n\n```\n## Extensions\n\n| Term     | URI                        |\n|----------|----------------------------|\n| `type`   | dxos.spec/ext/type@1.0     |\n| `feat`   | dxos.spec/ext/feat@1.0     |\n```\n\nAn extension definition is either:\n- **External** — resolved by URI (registry lookup, future tooling).\n- **Inline** — defined in the document's Appendix using `ext` blocks.\n\nA document may use both: external URIs for standard extensions,\ninline `ext` blocks for custom or experimental ones.\n\n---\n\n## Dependencies Section\n\nA spec lists the other specs it builds on under `## Dependencies`, after the opening prose and\nbefore `## Extensions`, one `uses` block per spec. A Composer plugin's runtime dependencies are the\nexception: they go in its `plugin` block's `dependsOn` (plugin dialect) and are never repeated under\n`uses`. The identifier is the depended-on spec's `id`;\nthe inline prose is required and says what this spec takes from it.\n\n```mdl\next uses\n  uri: dxos.spec.core/uses\n  desc: Declares that this spec builds on another spec, identified by its frontmatter id.\n  inline-prose: true\n  fields:\n    id: URI                     # the depended-on spec's id (the block identifier)\n  syntax: |\n    uses org.dxos.ui.react-ui-canvas: The engine whose types and operations this canvas models.\n```\n\nA reference into another spec is `<spec id>#<item>`, where the item is any block id or `req` id.\n\n---\n\n## The `ext` Primitive\n\n`ext` is the only block type the core language natively provides.\nIt is self-describing — defined here using itself:\n\n```mdl\next ext\n  uri: dxos.spec.core/ext\n  desc: Declares a new block type or extends an existing one.\n  fields:\n    uri:            URI           # canonical identifier for this extension (required)\n    desc:           Prose         # human description (required)\n    extends?:       ExtRef        # parent extension — inherits all parent fields\n    fields?:        FieldMap      # fields valid on instances of this block type\n    adds-fields?:   FieldMap      # additional fields merged into parent (use with extends)\n    nesting?:       self | none   # whether instances may contain same-type blocks\n    inline-prose?:  Boolean       # first non-key line treated as prose value\n    role?:          anchor | structure | verification | context   # how a map groups the kind\n    label?:         Identifier    # a numbered kind's label prefix; ids are keys, labels positional\n    hue?:           Identifier    # the kind's colour, a palette hue name\n    icon?:          Identifier    # the kind's icon, a Phosphor icon class\n    syntax?:        CodeBlock     # reference syntax (non-normative)\n    example?:       CodeBlock     # usage example (non-normative)\n```\n\n---\n\n## Primitive Types\n\nThe following types are available in `ext` field declarations.\nThey are the vocabulary of the meta-language.\n\n| Type         | Description                                                  |\n|--------------|--------------------------------------------------------------|\n| `Prose`      | Free-form text, single or multi-line                         |\n| `Identifier` | Alphanumeric name, hyphens allowed — e.g. `Board`, `F-1`    |\n| `URI`        | Dot-path or URL identifier — e.g. `dxos.spec/ext/type@1.0`  |\n| `SemVer`     | Semantic version string — e.g. `1.0.0`                      |\n| `Boolean`    | `true` or `false`                                            |\n| `TypeExpr`   | Type reference or inline type — e.g. `Color`, `Piece?`       |\n| `FieldMap`   | Named fields: `name[?]: TypeExpr  # comment`                 |\n| `UnionList`  | Pipe-separated literals: `a \\| b \\| c`                       |\n| `ProseList`  | Bulleted list of prose items                                 |\n| `CodeBlock`  | Literal indented or fenced text                              |\n| `ExtRef`     | Reference to a declared extension name                       |\n| `TagList`    | Space or comma-separated tag identifiers                     |\n\n---\n\n## Resolution Rules\n\n1. When a block type appears in a document body, the linter looks it up\n   in the nearest enclosing Extensions table (this document or any ancestor).\n2. If the URI resolves to an inline `ext` block in an Appendix, that definition is used.\n3. If the URI is external, the linter fetches the definition from a registry.\n4. An unresolvable block type is a lint error.\n5. Field names not declared in the `ext` definition are lint warnings.\n\n---\n\n## Non-Goals\n\nThe core language deliberately omits:\n\n- Execution semantics — `.mdl` is a specification, not a program.\n- Type checking — field values are not verified against TypeExpr at the core level.\n- Ordering constraints — sections may appear in any order.\n- Verification — requirements are semi-formal; the language is not a proof system.\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.features": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.features\nname: DSL.Features\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n  - org.dxos.spec.changelog\n---\n\nThe feature map dialect. A feature map is the top of the chain that turns intent into working code:\n\n| Layer       | Artifact                          | Who writes it            | What it says                         |\n| ----------- | --------------------------------- | ------------------------ | ------------------------------------ |\n| Feature map | `FEATURES.mdl`, `feature` blocks  | a person, or an agent    | what the product should do, and why  |\n| Spec        | `PLUGIN.mdl`, `SPEC.mdl`          | agent, reviewed in a PR  | how one package meets the feature    |\n| Review      | `<SPEC>.review.mdl`, `verdict`s   | agent (spec-review)      | how far the code meets each spec item |\n| Tasks       | `TASKS.md` beside the spec        | agent                    | the work that closes each gap        |\n| Code        | sources and tests                 | agent                    | the behaviour                        |\n| Result      | the re-run review, the feature's status | agent             | that the feature now holds           |\n\nA feature is a goal state (\"Feeds drop entries older than their retention\"); it names the spec items\nthat state it and does not restate them. Its status is authored, and `deus features check` derives a\nsecond status from the reviews of the items it cites, so a claim the code does not support shows up\nas a warning. The shape matches plugin-projects' `Feature` (`org.dxos.type.feature`), so a map can be\nloaded into a project's feature board.\n\nA feature map is a spec: every change adds a decision to its `## Changelog`, so the map records who\nasked for each feature and who moved its status.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term      | URI                        |\n| --------- | -------------------------- |\n| `feature` | `org.dxos.mdl.feature@1.0` |\n\n## Statuses\n\n| Status        | Meaning                                              | Derived from the reviews when      |\n| ------------- | ---------------------------------------------------- | ---------------------------------- |\n| `planned`     | Wanted; no work started.                             | every cited item is `missing`      |\n| `in-progress` | Partly built: some of what it cites works, some not. | any mix that is not one of these   |\n| `prototype`   | It works but is not relied on: nothing tests it.     | every item is `done` or `untested` |\n| `operational` | It works and tests hold it.                          | every item is `done`               |\n| `failing`     | It was operational and does not work now.            | never; a person or a QA run sets it |\n\nAn item with no verdict makes the derived status `unreviewed`, and the check compares nothing.\n\n## Spec references\n\n`specs:` lists repo-relative paths. A path alone cites every reviewable item of the spec; `#<id>`\ncites one block, and a `feat` with `req` items stands for its requirements.\n\n```text\npackages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl          # the whole spec\npackages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-1      # feat F-1, judged through F-1.1, F-1.2, ...\npackages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-2.3    # one requirement\npackages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#trim     # an op, type or component\n```\n\n## Checks\n\n`deus features check` reports, per feature, `<id> <status> <derived> <done>/<items> done`, then:\n\n- **errors** — an unknown status, a feature that cites no spec, a spec or item that does not exist,\n  a `depends` on a feature not on the map, and a dependency cycle;\n- **warnings** — a status the reviews do not support, a spec with no review or one that changed after\n  its review's commit, a feature `blocked` by a planned or failing dependency, one `operational`\n  ahead of a dependency, and an `in-progress` feature with no `## <feature id>` section in a\n  `TASKS.md` beside its spec (\"no work planned\": a gap nobody has planned to close).\n\n`deus features tasks <id>` prints the open items of one feature, each with its verdict and the first\nsentence of the verdict's note, as the start of that `TASKS.md` section.\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next feature\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feature@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A goal state of the product. The identifier is a stable kebab-case id, dotted by area\n    (`echo.feed-retention`); the inline prose is the feature's name.\n  fields:\n    status: planned | in-progress | prototype | operational | failing\n    specs: RefList          # repo-relative spec paths, each optionally #<item id>\n    depends?: RefList       # ids of features this one needs first; the graph has no cycles\n    note?: Prose            # why the feature matters, or what is left\n  syntax: |\n    feature echo.feed-retention: Feeds drop old entries\n      status: planned\n      specs: [packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-1, packages/core/echo/feed/SPEC.mdl#F-2]\n      depends: [echo.feed-store]\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.plugin": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.plugin\nname: DSL.Plugin\nversion: 1.1\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe plugin dialect: one `plugin` block that names a Composer plugin, says what it is for, and lists\nthe modules it contributes. Before this dialect a plugin was implied by its document (`PLUGIN.mdl`)\nand its frontmatter `id`; the block makes the plugin an item the spec can describe, review and link\nto like any other.\n\nIt is deliberately small. A module is listed by name with one line of prose; the capabilities it\nprovides, when it activates and what it requires are left to the code and to later versions of the\ndialect. Use the app dialect (`app.mdl`) for the plugin's UI: graph nodes, surfaces and menus.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term     | URI                        |\n| -------- | -------------------------- |\n| `plugin` | `org.dxos.mdl.plugin@1.1` |\n\n## Model\n\n- A plugin spec opens with its `plugin` block, directly after the frontmatter and before any\n  prose, so the first thing a reader sees is what the plugin is and what it adds. A document\n  declares at most one, and its identifier is the plugin's class name (`CodePlugin`). `id:`\n  repeats the frontmatter `id`, so the block can be quoted on its own.\n- `dependsOn:` is optional and lists the plugin ids this plugin needs at runtime, exactly as\n  `dx.config.ts` declares them. Each is also the `id` in that plugin's `PLUGIN.mdl` frontmatter.\n  It is written inline (`dependsOn: [a, b]`) or as a block list (`dependsOn:` then indented\n  `- id` lines, as `modules:`). Specs it only builds on or refers to go under `uses` instead.\n- `modules:` lists every module the plugin adds (`Plugin.addModule` in its `plugin.ts`). Each\n  item is the module's export name, a colon, and one line on what it contributes.\n- A module named here is a spec item: a review can judge it (`verdict ReactSurface`), and a\n  `github:` key may point at it (`…/src/capabilities/index.ts#ReactSurface`).\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next plugin\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.plugin@1.1\n  desc: |\n    A Composer plugin: what it is for and the modules it adds. The identifier is the plugin's\n    class name; the inline prose is its purpose in one line.\n  fields:\n    id: URI                     # the plugin id, as in the document's frontmatter\n    desc?: Prose                # what the plugin is for, and what it leaves to other plugins\n    dependsOn?: URI[]           # runtime plugin dependencies, as in dx.config.ts\n    modules: ProseList          # `- Name: what it contributes`, one per Plugin.addModule\n  syntax: |\n    plugin CodePlugin: Authors and reviews DEUS specs.\n      id: org.dxos.plugin.code\n      dependsOn: [org.dxos.plugin.illustrator]\n      modules:\n        - ReactSurface: the spec article and section surfaces\n        - SpecSync: two-way sync of specs with files on disk\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.qa": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.qa\nname: DSL.QA\nversion: 1.1\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe QA dialect. Adds the executable half of a specification, so a `.mdl` document doubles as a\ntest plan that a human tester and an agent tester execute from the same source.\n\nThe vocabulary is borrowed, not invented, and comes in two layers:\n\n| Layer | Term | Meaning |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Gherkin — criteria, derivable | `feat` / `req` | a feature and its requirements |\n| | `scenario T-n` | given / when / then; a criterion, not a script (Deus.Std) |\n| xUnit — execution, runnable | `suite` | a named set of tests, selected by name or tag |\n| | `test QA-n` | one executable case: `before` / `steps` / `after` |\n| | `step` | one move: a human instruction and, usually, one operation |\n\nA `test` is an ordered list of steps with a success criterion per step. Each step carries two\nfaces: a human instruction and, where the step maps to an invocable operation, an agent binding.\nNeither face is a rendering of the other — a human clicks a navtree row, an agent invokes an\noperation, and both are the truth about how that actor performs the step.\n\nTests live in a `## QA` section of a `PLUGIN.mdl`, or in `spec/APP.mdl` of an application for\njourneys that cross plugins. Suites live beside the tests they group.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term    | URI                        |\n| ------- | -------------------------- |\n| `test`  | `org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0`    |\n| `step`  | `org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0`    |\n| `suite` | `org.dxos.mdl.suite@1.0`   |\n| `op`    | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`      |\n\n`org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0` named the given/when/then block until 2026-09-08; that block is now\n`scenario` (`org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`) and `test` is the executable one. A document declaring\n`test` in its Extensions table means this definition.\n\n---\n\n## Relationship to `scenario`\n\n`scenario` and `test` are not competitors and must not be collapsed:\n\n| | `scenario T-n` | `test QA-n` |\n|---|---|---|\n| Kind | derivable criterion | orchestrable script |\n| Shape | given / when / then | N ordered steps in three stages |\n| Target | vitest, storybook play | the running app |\n| Asserted by | the test runner | the human, or an agent through the debug port |\n\nA test's `covers:` names the `feat` / `req` / `scenario` identifiers it exercises, so coverage is\na question the linter can answer rather than a guess.\n\n---\n\n## The `test` Block\n\n```mdl\next test\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.test@1.0\n  desc: |\n    An end-to-end case against the running application, executed by a human tester or an\n    agent. Three stages of ordered steps, each step with its own success criterion.\n  fields:\n    desc?:     Prose\n    actors?:   human | agent | both     # default both; see the invoke rule below\n    covers?:   RefList                  # feat / req / scenario ids this test exercises\n    status?:   passing | failing | blocked | unverified   # last known result, with a date in a comment\n    automated?: RefList                 # playwright specs that execute this test unattended (see below)\n    given?:    GivenList                # preconditions; `name: prose` binds `$given.name` (see below)\n    before?:   StepList                 # fixture the test needs, but is not itself testing\n    steps:     StepList                 # the test — the steps whose failure is a defect\n    after?:    StepList                 # returning the app to its pre-test state\n                                        # all three are arrays of the same `step` shape\n    then?:     ProseList                # whole-test criteria, beyond the per-step ones\n    tags?:     TagList\n  nesting: none\n```\n\n```mdl\next step\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.step@1.0\n  desc: |\n    One move in a test — an item of a stage list, not a block of its own. `do` and `expect`\n    are required: that is what keeps a test human-runnable, and a step no human can perform is a\n    design smell rather than a shortcut. A step invokes at most one operation.\n  fields:\n    name?:     Prose          # short title, for the report table\n    id?:       Identifier     # stable handle; see the numbering rule below\n    do:        Prose          # the human instruction (required)\n    expect:    Prose          # the success criterion BOTH actors judge (required)\n    invoke?:   OpInvocation   # operation key + input literal; one operation per step\n    space?:    Expr           # the space the invoker receives; default `$given.space`, else the default space\n    capture?:  Identifier     # names this step's result for later steps as `$<name>`\n    assert?:   CodeBlock      # deterministic check evaluated in the debug port; must return truthy\n    note?:     Prose          # a constraint the runner must respect (see Execution Rules)\n    blocked?:  Prose          # why this step cannot run at all, and what must change first\n```\n\nSteps are items of a list, not repeated `step <n>:` keys. Core declares block bodies as\n`key[?]: value`, with `key:` followed by an indented `-` list as the multi-line form — which\n`given` already uses. A pseudo-key carrying a positional id is not core syntax, and\nit forces every later step to be renumbered by hand when one is inserted.\n\n### Field rules\n\n1. **`do` and `expect` are required on every step.** A test whose steps only an agent can perform\n   is not a QA plan, it is a script.\n2. **`invoke` is optional, and its absence is meaningful.** A step with no operation behind it —\n   a drag, a page reload, a judgement about flicker — is a first-class step. A test containing any\n   such step cannot declare `actors: agent`.\n3. **`assert` is optional.** `expect` prose is always the criterion; `assert` only makes it\n   deterministic where that is cheap. Requiring it would make authoring a test a coding task.\n4. **`capture` threads a result forward** as `$<name>`, referenced in a later step's `invoke`.\n   Within a step, `$result` is that step's own `invoke` result, for use in its `assert`, and\n   `$<name>.snapshot` is the snapshot taken after that step, for an operation that returns less\n   than the step produced. Only serializable values cross a snippet boundary — an id does, a live\n   ECHO object does not (Rule 2).\n5. **`$snapshot` is the app's state after the step.** The runner takes one\n   `org.dxos.operation.debug.snapshot` after every step's `invoke` (or, for a step with none,\n   after the human reports it done) and binds it as `$snapshot` for the step's `assert`. It\n   carries `layout`, `planks`, `spaces`, `toasts`, `surfaces`, `attention`, `plugins`, and the\n   `errors` logged since the run started. A step judges the app by its snapshot or by a query\n   operation, never by the `invoke` return value (Rule 3). When a test needs something the snapshot\n   does not report, extend the snapshot operation rather than scripting the page.\n   Alongside it the runner binds **`$stepErrors`** — the entries of `$snapshot.errors` logged since\n   the previous step's snapshot, so this step's own errors and no one else's. `$snapshot.errors` is\n   cumulative by design (Rule 11) and therefore useless as a pass/fail clause: one background error\n   early in a run makes `$snapshot.errors.length === 0` false for every step after it. An `assert`\n   that judges errors uses `$stepErrors`; `$snapshot.errors` is for reporting.\n6. **A `given` entry written `name: prose` binds `$given.name`.** A bare entry is a condition to\n   check; a named one is also a value the runner must resolve and hold for the steps to reference\n   (`$given.collection`). Without this, a step's `invoke` would name something the test never\n   defined.\n7. **Steps are numbered by position**, from 1 within their stage, so `QA-1.3` is the third item\n   of `steps`. A step that needs a handle stable across insertions declares `id:`, and is then\n   referenced by it (`QA-1.open`) instead. Most steps do not need one; give an `id` to a step\n   whose failure is cited somewhere outside the document.\n\n### `automated` — the unattended arm of a test\n\nA `test QA-n` is the specification of a journey; a Playwright spec is one *execution* of it, run\nunattended on every CI cell. `automated:` names those specs, each entry\n`<package>:<file>#<full test title>`:\n\n```mdl\n  automated:\n    - composer-e2e:basic.spec.ts#Basic tests › create document\n```\n\nThe binding is declared on **both** sides and checked, rather than left as prose:\n`packages/e2e/composer-e2e/scripts/check-qa-coverage.mjs` reads every `automated:` here and every\n`@QA` tag in the specs, and fails when either side names something the other does not have. A\none-sided link rots silently — a renamed test keeps a green check while the flow it claimed to\ncover stops being exercised.\n\n1. **`automated` never replaces the steps.** The `do`/`expect` prose stays the contract, because a\n   spec asserts what it was written to assert and the flow is what a human re-runs when it breaks.\n   A test whose steps were deleted \"because Playwright covers it\" is no longer a QA flow.\n2. **The spec is free to cover a subset**, and usually does: `after` is teardown the runner does\n   not need when every worker gets a fresh profile, and a step with no operation behind it (a drag,\n   a reload) may be the whole reason a flow is `actors: human`. Coverage is a claim about the\n   `steps`, not about every stage.\n3. **A spec may name several flows** and a flow several specs; both sides are lists.\n4. **The check is referential, not semantic.** It proves the two sides name each other and that the\n   named test runs; it cannot read a Playwright spec and decide whether it exercises these `steps`.\n   A test tagged `@QA-n` that asserts nothing still passes the check, so the claim that a spec\n   covers a flow is a reviewer's judgement, not a verified fact. What the flow's own `assert`s\n   verify is the `composer-qa` run, which executes these steps against the live app; nothing\n   cross-checks the two arms against each other.\n\n### Input literals\n\nAn `invoke` is an operation key followed by a JSON-shaped input. Inside it:\n\n| Form | Meaning | Snippet the runner emits |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| `$name`, `$name.field`, `$name.snapshot.…` | a captured result or a `given` binding; any JS path over it | the held value |\n| `$runId` | this run's id, so artifacts are attributable | the string |\n| `Obj(\"echo://S/O\")` | the live object at that URI | `dxos.spaces('S').db.getObjectById('O')` |\n| `Ref(\"echo://S/O\")` | a reference to it, for `Ref` inputs | `dxos.Ref.make(…getObjectById('O'))` |\n| `Space(\"S\")` | the live space, for `space` inputs | `dxos.spaces('S')` |\n\nPlaceholders may appear inside strings (`\"QA: Basics $runId\"`, `\"root/$given.space.id/…\"`). A\n`create` operation returns a URI, not an object, precisely so that a later step can name the\nobject through `Obj(…)` after a snippet boundary. A held value that is an `echo://` URI also\nexposes `.spaceId` and `.objectId`, parsed by the runner, so a navigation path is written\n`\"root/$space.id/content/collections/$doc.objectId\"` without a step to split the URI.\n\n### Operation references\n\nWithin the document, an operation declared here is referenced as `[op:<id>]`, which resolves via\nthat block's `key:` field (`op@1.1`).\n\nAn operation owned by **another** plugin is written as a full key literal. This is not only an\n`APP.mdl` concern: a plugin-scoped test routinely drives `space.addObject` and `appToolkit.open`,\nand neither is declared in the plugin's own spec.\n\n---\n\n## The `suite` Block\n\n```mdl\next suite\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.suite@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A named set of tests, run together and reported together. A container by reference: the\n    tests stay standalone blocks, and a suite lists them by id — `QA-1` for a test in the same\n    document, `markdown:QA-1` for another plugin's (`app:QA-1` for the application spec).\n  fields:\n    desc?:   Prose\n    tags?:   TagList        # `run --tag nightly` selects every suite carrying the tag\n    tests:   RefList        # ordered for the report; execution is order-independent\n  nesting: none\n```\n\n1. **Suites are order-independent by construction.** Every test owns its fixture (`given` +\n   `before`) and its teardown (`after`), so any selection of tests is runnable in any order. There\n   is no suite-level `before`: anything every member needs — a plugin enabled, an identity — goes in\n   each test's `given`, which the runner establishes idempotently.\n2. **Every document is an implicit suite** of its own tests, addressed by its short name\n   (`run markdown`), so a plugin needs no `suite` block to be runnable as a unit.\n3. **Tags select suites, not tests.** `run --tag nightly` runs the union of the tests of every\n   suite tagged `nightly`, de-duplicated. A test's own `tags:` are descriptive and searchable but\n   do not put it in a run.\n\n---\n\n## `op@1.1` — the `key` field\n\n```mdl\next op\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1\n  extends: op\n  adds-fields:\n    key?: NSID    # runtime operation key — what the invoker receives\n```\n\nWithout `key`, a test cannot say which operation it means. The distinction is real and not\nacademic: `plugin-markdown` declares one `op create` but ships two runtime operations —\n`org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` (agent-facing, `Database.Service`) and\n`org.dxos.operation.markdown.createDraft` (the UI factory, `Capability.Service`). A test that\nguesses picks the wrong one and fails at runtime.\n\nThe spec mirrors the code: one `op` block per runtime `Operation.make` key, with `key:` and\n`requires:` copied from its `meta.key` and `services`. A design-level operation with no runtime\ncounterpart keeps its block without `key:` and says so in `status: unimplemented`, so a reader can\ntell an intended operation from an existing one and a test never references it.\n\nPopulate the pre-existing `requires:` from the code's `services:` at the same time. It is\ninformational — see Execution Rule 1 — but it tells a reader why a step behaves as it does.\n\n---\n\n## Execution Rules\n\nThese are not style advice. Each was established by running a test against a live application;\na runner that ignores them produces false failures.\n\n1. **Always invoke through the operation invoker with an explicit `spaceId`.** Do not branch on\n   `requires:`. An operation's own declaration cannot see the services its *downstream* calls\n   need, so `requires:` is necessary but not sufficient to predict whether a plain invoke will\n   work. Taking the invoker path unconditionally removes the question. The step's `space:` names\n   the space; absent, `$given.space`, else the default space.\n\n2. **Coalesce adjacent steps that thread a live object.** The debug port serializes between\n   snippets, so an ECHO object captured in one step cannot be passed to the next. Human step\n   granularity and snippet granularity are different things, and reconciling them is the runner's\n   job, not the author's. Prefer captures that are strings (a URI, an id, a path) and `Obj(…)` in\n   the next step, which needs no coalescing at all.\n\n3. **Verify the effect, never the return value.** A successful invocation frequently did less\n   than the step intended — a `create` operation is a factory and places nothing. Judge `expect`\n   against `$snapshot`, a query operation, or the DOM.\n\n4. **Establish `given` or abort.** A test that runs against unmet preconditions reports failures\n   that belong to the fixture, not the application.\n\n5. **Assertions must be falsifiable against a dirty fixture.** An existence-shaped check\n   (`some((o) => o.name === 'Notes')`) is already true when a previous run left its artifacts\n   behind, so the step reports pass having done nothing. Assert on the identity the test just\n   produced (`o.id === $created.object.id`), and name the test's own artifacts in `given` as\n   preconditions of their absence. Artifacts carry the `QA:` prefix and `$runId` in their name.\n\n6. **Resolve an operation by its exact key, never by suffix.** `endsWith('create')` matches both\n   `org.dxos.operation.markdown.create` and `org.dxos.operation.markdown.createDraft` — the very\n   ambiguity `key:` exists to remove.\n\n7. **Three stages, run in order: `before`, `steps`, `after` — each an array of the same `step`.**\n   Put a step in `before` only when it is fixture the test is not itself exercising. Creating the\n   subject usually IS the test: QA-2 tests that two documents can be made and linked, so the\n   creating belongs in `steps`, and `before` stays empty. `before` builds the fixture,\n   `steps` is the test, `after` undoes it. The split is what makes a partial run meaningful — and\n   what makes a failure legible, since a `before` failure is a broken fixture while a `steps`\n   failure is a defect in the application. Every stage is a step list; `after` in particular is\n   steps, not prose, and goes through operations like any other. A prose instruction (\"delete the\n   document\") leaves each actor to pick a mechanism, and they diverge: a human uses the navtree's\n   delete action, which routes through `space.removeObjects` and closes any open plank; a runner\n   reaching for `space.db.remove` strands a plank pointing at an object that no longer exists,\n   which the user cannot then close. `after` removes only what this run created, by identity.\n\n8. **A partial run is the runner's option, not the test's.** A test always declares all three\n   stages; which of them to run is decided per invocation. Running `before` alone stands a fixture\n   up to look at; `after` alone tears one down; `steps` alone re-tests against a fixture already\n   standing. Whatever is skipped must be reported — what remains and how to remove it — since the\n   next full run's `given` will otherwise fail on its own leftovers (Rule 5).\n\n9. **A step that cannot run says so in `blocked`, and the test's `status` becomes `blocked`.**\n   Not `failing` — nothing was tested — and not silence, which reads as untried. A duplicate\n   operation key, a missing capability or a removed operation all land here.\n\n10. **Report by test and step number** — `QA-1.3`, or `QA-1.<id>` for a step declaring one — so a\n    failure is addressable without prose. Prefer the id where a step has one: it survives a step\n    being inserted ahead of it, a position does not.\n\n11. **Snapshot after every step, and `errors` is always a finding.** The runner passes the run's\n    start time as `since`, so each snapshot reports every error logged so far; a non-empty\n    `errors` fails nothing by itself but is recorded against the step where it first appeared,\n    even when that step's own check passed. A step that means to fail on an error asserts on\n    `$stepErrors` (Field rule 5), never on `$snapshot.errors`: the cumulative array keeps one early\n    background error false for the rest of the run, which reads as every later step failing for\n    something none of them did. Both smoke runs of `app:QA-1` hit exactly that.\n\n12. **A suite run continues past a failed test.** Each test is self-contained, so a failure in\n    one — including a failed `after` — is reported and the next test's `given` guards it. A run\n    stops early only when the app itself is gone: the port no longer answers, or the page has\n    crashed.\n\n---\n\n## Example\n\n```mdl\ntest QA-1: Create a space and a document, then delete the document\n  actors: both\n  covers: [F-1.1, T-1]\n  tags: [smoke]\n  given:\n    - an identity exists and the default space is SPACE_READY\n    - no space whose name starts with \"QA:\" exists — ABORT rather than deleting one this test did not create\n  before:\n    - name: Create a space\n      do: Open the space list's +, name the space \"QA: Basics $runId\" and create it.\n      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.create { name: \"QA: Basics $runId\" }\n      capture: space\n      expect: the navtree switches to the new space\n      assert: return $snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.id === $space.id && s.state === 'SPACE_READY')\n  steps:\n    - name: Create a document\n      do: With the new space selected, click + and choose Document. Name it \"QA: Doc 1\".\n      invoke: org.dxos.operation.markdown.create { name: \"QA: Doc 1\", content: \"# QA: Doc 1\\n\" }\n      space: $space.id\n      capture: doc\n      expect: \"QA: Doc 1\" appears as a row under the space\n      assert: |\n        const docs = await composer.invoke('org.dxos.operation.space.queryObjects',\n          { typename: 'org.dxos.type.document' }, { spaceId: $space.id });\n        return docs.some((o) => o.id === $doc.id);\n    - name: Delete it\n      do: Open the row's ⋮ menu and choose Delete.\n      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.removeObjects { objects: [Obj($doc.id)] }\n      space: $space.id\n      expect: a \"Document deleted\" toast with Undo appears and the row is gone\n      assert: return $snapshot.toasts.some((t) => t.title === 'Document deleted') && $stepErrors.length === 0\n  after:\n    - name: Delete the space\n      do: Open the space's settings and delete the space; pick the default space.\n      invoke: org.dxos.operation.space.delete { space: Space($space.id) }\n      expect: no \"QA:\" space remains and the workspace is the default space\n      assert: return !$snapshot.spaces.some((s) => s.name?.startsWith('QA:'))\n```\n\n```mdl\nsuite smoke: Boots, one space, one document\n  desc: The five-minute check a Routine runs on every merge to main.\n  tags: [smoke, nightly]\n  tests: [QA-1]\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.review": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.review\nname: DSL.Review\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe review dialect. A review is a separate document that judges how far the code meets a spec, item\nby item. It is written by an agent (the `spec-review` skill) or a person, and lives beside the spec\nit judges: `PLUGIN.mdl` → `PLUGIN.review.mdl`. Each new review replaces the previous file; git keeps\nthe history. The spec viewer reads the latest review and marks each judged item with its status.\n\nA review is evidence, not a decision: it never changes the spec, and it adds no changelog entry.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term      | URI                         |\n| --------- | --------------------------- |\n| `verdict` | `org.dxos.mdl.verdict@1.0` |\n\n## Document\n\nFrontmatter names what was reviewed and against what:\n\n```yaml\n---\nspec: github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/PLUGIN.mdl\ncommit: 64dcf3f44a541fdf0b3e55803f38d214e76a6279   # the code the review read\ndate: 2026-10-22\nby: agent:claude-code                              # same actor forms as the changelog\n---\n```\n\nThe body opens with a short summary, then one `verdict` per reviewed item.\n\n## Statuses\n\n| Status     | Meaning                                                                  |\n| ---------- | ------------------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| `done`     | The code does what the item says.                                        |\n| `untested` | The code does it, but no test exercises it.                              |\n| `partial`  | Some of what the item says is done; the note says which part is not.     |\n| `diverged` | The code does something different from what the item says.               |\n| `missing`  | Nothing in the code does it.                                             |\n\nThe spec viewer shows beside each judged item whether code covers it (its `evidence:`, or \"no code\")\nand whether tests exercise it: `tested` when the verdict names `tests:` or is `done`, `untested` for\nany other status but `missing`, where there is no code to test.\n\n## Tests and scenarios\n\nA `scenario` or a `test` block describes behaviour to exercise, so it is judged on whether\nautomation exercises it, not on whether code implements it:\n\n| Status     | For a `scenario` or `test`                                                  |\n| ---------- | --------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `done`     | An automated test runs it end to end; `tests:` cites that test.             |\n| `partial`  | A test covers some of its steps or `then:` clauses; the note says which.    |\n| `diverged` | A test runs something different from what it describes.                     |\n| `missing`  | Nothing runs it (a manual QA run is not automation).                        |\n\n`untested` does not apply. `tests:` names the test files that exercise it and is required for\n`done`, `partial` and `diverged`; `evidence:` may name the code it drives. The viewer shows\n`tested`, `partly tested` or `not tested` beside it, with a chip per test file.\n\n## Walkthrough\n\nWhen an item is implemented in more than one place, the verdict carries a `walkthrough:`: 1 to 5\nsegments, in reading order, that say where each part is implemented. Each segment is one\nsentence followed by the `github:` key of the code it describes; the viewer shows the sentence\nabove the declaration the key names, linked to its file. Keep each sentence to what the code\ndoes for this item, not how it works line by line.\n\n- A `type` needs none: its one declaration is the hover card of its code chip.\n- An `op` walks its declaration (the schema), then each handler.\n- A `feat` or `req` walks the pieces in the order a request passes through them.\n\nAn item with a single implementation gets no walkthrough.\n\nA `feat` with nested `req` items is judged through its requirements; its status is the worst of\ntheirs. Judge a `feat` directly only when it has no requirements.\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next verdict\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.verdict@1.0\n  desc: |\n    How far the code meets one spec item. The identifier is the item's id in the reviewed spec\n    (`F-1.2`, `Scene`, `openDirectory`); the inline prose is the verdict in one line.\n  fields:\n    status: done | untested | partial | diverged | missing\n    evidence?: RefList          # github: keys of the code that supports the verdict, with #Symbol\n    tests?: RefList             # github: keys of the test files that exercise the item, without #\n    walkthrough?: ProseList     # 1-5 items `- <one sentence>. github:<key>`, when there is more than one implementation\n    note?: Prose                # what is missing or different; required unless status is done\n  syntax: |\n    verdict F-1.2: The picker opens on the action.\n      status: done\n      evidence: [github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/src/util.ts#openDirectoryPicker]\n      tests: [github:dxos/dxos/packages/plugins/plugin-file-system/src/util.test.ts]\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.software": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.software\nname: DSL.Software\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n  - org.dxos.spec.changelog\n---\n\nThe software dialect: the block kinds a package spec (`PLUGIN.mdl`, `SPEC.mdl`) describes code\nwith. Specs used to repeat these definitions in their appendix; a spec that extends this dialect\ngets them from here, and an appendix `ext` block still adds or overrides a kind for that spec.\n\nEvery `ext` block below is also the schema the spec editor builds a block's form from.\n\n## Schema fields\n\nBeyond the core `ext` fields, a definition may state how tools present and connect its blocks:\n\n| Field    | Meaning                                                                                |\n| -------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |\n| `role`   | `anchor`, `structure`, `verification` or `context`: how a map groups the kind.         |\n| `label`  | A numbered kind's label prefix (`F`). Its blocks carry a stable key as their id, and   |\n|          | tools draw the positional label (`F-1`, `F-1.2`) instead of writing it.                |\n| `hue`    | The kind's colour, a palette hue name (`emerald`).                                     |\n| `icon`   | The kind's icon, a Phosphor icon class (`ph--star--regular`).                          |\n\nA field whose type is `Ref<kind>` or `Ref<kind>[]` points at blocks of that kind; `Ref<spec>`\npoints at another spec. Reference fields are what maps draw as edges and what a spec's nesting is\ncomputed from: a `feat` block's `spec:` names the spec that breaks it down.\n\nA `RequirementList` field holds keyed lines named after the field (`req <key>: prose`), each with\nits own key and label.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term        | URI                           |\n| ----------- | ----------------------------- |\n| `type`      | `org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0`       |\n| `op`        | `org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1`         |\n| `feat`      | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.1`       |\n| `scenario`  | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`   |\n| `component` | `org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0`  |\n| `service`   | `org.dxos.mdl.service@1.0`    |\n| `module`    | `org.dxos.mdl.module@1.0`     |\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next type\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.type@1.0\n  desc: A named data structure with typed fields and optional literals.\n  role: structure\n  hue: blue\n  icon: ph--cube--regular\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    fields?: FieldMap        # name[?]: TypeExpr  (# inline comment)\n    literals?: UnionList     # a | b | c\n    extends?: Ref<type>[]\n```\n\n```mdl\next op\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.op@1.1\n  desc: A named operation with typed inputs, outputs, and declared errors.\n  role: structure\n  hue: orange\n  icon: ph--lightning--regular\n  fields:\n    key?: NSID                  # runtime operation key: what the invoker receives\n    desc?: Prose\n    input?: FieldMap            # named input parameters\n    output?: TypeExpr           # return type\n    errors?: ErrorMap           # name: Prose  (when this error occurs)\n    effects?: EffectList        # echo:read | echo:write | http | fs | ...\n    requires?: Ref<service>[]   # injected service dependencies\n    note?: Prose                # implementation guidance (non-normative)\n```\n\n```mdl\next feat\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.1\n  desc: A named feature grouping one or more requirements.\n  role: anchor\n  label: F\n  hue: emerald\n  icon: ph--star--regular\n  inline-prose: true\n  fields:\n    status?: planned | in-progress | prototype | operational | failing\n    desc?: Prose\n    needs?: Ref<feat>[]         # features this one needs first; no cycles\n    spec?: Ref<spec>            # the spec that breaks this feature down\n    req: RequirementList        # req <key>: one testable sentence\n```\n\n```mdl\next scenario\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0\n  desc: An acceptance scenario expressed as given / when / then steps.\n  role: verification\n  hue: cyan\n  icon: ph--path--regular\n  fields:\n    given?: Step | Step[]\n    when?: Step | Step[]\n    then: Step | Step[]\n    tags?: TagList\n```\n\n```mdl\next component\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.component@1.0\n  desc: A UI component with props, internal state, slots, actions, and events.\n  role: structure\n  hue: pink\n  icon: ph--browser--regular\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    props?: FieldMap            # external inputs (immutable inside component)\n    state?: FieldMap            # internal reactive state\n    slots?: FieldMap            # named ReactNode injection points\n    actions?: ActionMap         # methods the component exposes or handles\n    emits?: EventMap            # events the component raises to its parent\n    layout?: CodeBlock          # ASCII sketch of visual structure (non-normative)\n```\n\n```mdl\next service\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.service@1.0\n  desc: An external or system-level dependency providing named operations.\n  role: structure\n  hue: yellow\n  icon: ph--plug--regular\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    loading?: eager | lazy\n    ops?: OpMap                 # name(params) → ReturnType\n    errors?: ErrorMap\n    note?: Prose\n```\n\n```mdl\next module\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.module@1.0\n  desc: An ESM namespace module, re-exported from index.ts as `export * as <Name> from './<Name>'`.\n  role: structure\n  hue: indigo\n  icon: ph--package--regular\n  fields:\n    desc?: Prose\n    api: ApiMap                 # public members reached as `<Name>.<member>`\n```\n",
  "org.dxos.spec.sql": "---\nid: org.dxos.spec.sql\nname: DSL.Sql\nversion: 1.0\nextends:\n  - org.dxos.spec.core\n---\n\nThe SQL dialect: one `table` block that declares a SQL table a component owns — its columns, key,\nindexes, and the migration set that creates it. Before this dialect a spec named its tables only in\nprose (\"a new migration adds the column\"), so a reviewer could not see the schema a requirement\nrelies on, and a spec review could not judge it.\n\nA `table` block states the schema as it is after the spec's changes, not the history: the history is\nthe numbered migrations in the code, and the reasons are in the changelog. Use `type` for an ECHO\nschema; a `table` is a store-level SQL table that is not an ECHO object.\n\n## Extensions\n\n| Term    | URI                       |\n| ------- | ------------------------- |\n| `table` | `org.dxos.mdl.table@1.0` |\n\n## Model\n\n- **The identifier is the table name**, exactly as in the DDL (`feeds`, `convergenceKeyIntents`).\n  The inline prose says what one row is.\n- **`columns:`** has one line per column: `name: SQLTYPE [constraint …]  # meaning`.\n  - `SQLTYPE` is a SQLite storage class: `INTEGER`, `REAL`, `TEXT` or `BLOB`. A column that holds\n    JSON is `TEXT` with `json` in its comment and the TypeScript type it decodes to.\n  - A trailing `?` on the name means `NULL` is allowed (`feedNamespace?: TEXT`); without it the\n    column is `NOT NULL`.\n  - Constraints are written as in SQL: `PRIMARY KEY`, `AUTOINCREMENT`, `UNIQUE`, `DEFAULT <value>`,\n    `REFERENCES <table>(<column>)`.\n- **`key:`** names the primary key, and `unique:` any other unique keys, as column lists.\n- **`indexes:`** has one line per secondary index: `name(columns)` and why a query needs it.\n- **`store:`** names the database the table lives in and, in parentheses, its migration-history table\n  (`feed store (feed_migrations)`), so the reader knows which migrations change it. A spec names\n  components and specs, never file paths: the code moves, the names stay.\n- **`new:`** marks a table or columns the spec adds: `table` for a new table, or the list of new\n  columns. A spec review checks that a migration adds each of them.\n- A `table` is a spec item: a requirement cites it as `table:feeds` or a column as\n  `table:feeds.options`, a review can judge it (`verdict table:feeds`), and a `github:` key may\n  point at the migration that creates it.\n\n## Definitions\n\n```mdl\next table\n  uri: org.dxos.mdl.table@1.0\n  desc: |\n    A SQL table a component owns. The identifier is the table name; the inline prose is what one row\n    is. Columns, keys and indexes are the schema after the spec's changes.\n  fields:\n    store: Prose                # the database, and its migration-history table in parentheses\n    columns: ColumnMap          # `name[?]: SQLTYPE [constraint …]  # meaning`; `?` allows NULL\n    key?: Prose                 # the primary key, as a column list\n    unique?: ProseList          # other unique keys, as column lists\n    indexes?: ProseList         # `name(columns): why a query needs it`\n    new?: Prose                 # `table`, or the columns this spec adds\n  inline-prose: true\n  syntax: |\n    table feeds: One feed of a space, by namespace.\n      store: feed store (feed_migrations)\n      columns:\n        feedPrivateId: INTEGER PRIMARY KEY AUTOINCREMENT  # local row id; blocks reference it\n        spaceId: TEXT\n        feedId: TEXT                                      # the Feed object's entity id\n        options?: TEXT                                    # json FeedOptions; NULL = defaults\n      key: feedPrivateId\n      new: options\n```\n",
};
